  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how different optical and scanning probes can diagnose photocurrent generation and flow in a two-dimensional material, shown as a graphene-like honeycomb sheet. Light of different colors excites a broad region, representing photocurrent spectroscopy, while additional tools target more specific properties: wavy red beams indicate probes of quantum geometry, a tip above the sample suggests spin-sensitive detection, paired pulses at times \\(t\\) and \\(t+\\Delta t\\) illustrate ultrafast autocorrelation methods, and a nanoscale tip with a focused beam highlights near-field nanoscopy and current streamline microscopy.\n\nTogether, these methods are mapped onto the photocurrent life cycle. The lower schematics show the sequence from absorption of light and creation of excited carriers, to current generation, then relaxation and carrier dynamics, and finally current collection at contacts. The image emphasizes that no single technique captures everything; instead, complementary measurements reveal where light is absorbed, how currents are launched, how excited states scatter or relax, and how charge is ultimately funneled into measurable photocurrent. Overall, the figure presents a toolkit for spatially, temporally, and microscopically resolving the mechanisms behind photocurrent in quantum materials.",
